  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... An unsettled pattern will develop for the end of \nthe work week, with Friday afternoon into Saturday the most likely \ntime period for showers/storms.\n\nA cold front approaching the region will slow dramatically once it \nreaches central North Carolina early Friday, likely taking most of \nFriday and Saturday to make it the rest of the way to the coastline. \nWhile the precipitation shield will not remain completely on the \nwarm side of the front, expect anywhere between a 50-70% chance for \nshowers/storms on Friday, with the highest chances across the \nsoutheast. The entire forecast area is under a marginal (level 1 of \n5) risk for severe weather that day, which would primarily come from \ndamaging wind. While there is not much wind shear in the vertical \nprofile to help organize storms, there could be anywhere between \n1000-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE, depending on the model solution. In \naddition, there is a marginal (level 1 of 4) risk for excessive \nrainfall generally to the west of US-1 and a slight (level 2 of 4) \nrisk to the east of US-1. Not only will there not be much wind shear \nto organize storms, the wind itself will generally be light through \nthe profile, resulting in slow storm movement. As the front shifts \neast on Saturday, the chance of rain will drop to 20-70%, with \nsoutheastern locations still having the highest risk. The marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall will continue everywhere on Saturday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above normal temperatures return to the forecast by \nMonday and continue into the beginning of September.\n\nOnce the front slips offshore, an upper ridge will develop over the \nsoutheastern United States. This will bring the surface wind out of \nthe southwest or the west, with downsloping wind off the mountains. \nThis will bring a return of high temperatures to the mid 90s \nareawide. Record high temperatures are generally in the triple \ndigits, so record highs shouldn't be set during this time period. \nStill, it will be a hot and dry start to the month of September.",
